# Cross-Agent Git Rebase Guard (`cross-agent-git-rebase-guard`)

When multiple AI agents generate commits simultaneously in separate worktrees (`feat/argon-work` and `feat/fable-work`), merging branches naively can trigger interactive editor prompts, merge conflicts, or dirty working directory locks.

This skill governs non-interactive, automated branch merging and rebase synchronization.

---

## 1. Clean Synchronization Workflow

```
feat/argon-work  ----[ Commit A1 ]----[ Commit A2 ]----+
                                                       | (Clean Merge)
main             --------------------------------------+----> [ Unified Main ]
                                                       |
feat/fable-work  ----[ Commit F1 ]----[ Commit F2 ]----+
```

---

## 2. Non-Interactive Merge Commands

When integrating concurrent work back to `main`:

```bash
# 1. Switch to root repository
cd /path/to/project-root

# 2. Ensure working directory is clean
git status --porcelain

# 3. Merge Argon feature branch with automated commit message
git merge feat/argon-work --no-edit -m "merge: integrate Argon asset and LTX enhancements"

# 4. Merge Fable engine branch
git merge feat/fable-work --no-edit -m "merge: integrate Fable 64-bit C++ engine refactors"

# 5. Push unified master/main to remote
git push origin main
```

---

## 3. Conflict Prevention Guardrails

1. **Commit Early, Commit Atomic:** Each agent must commit atomic milestones rather than leaving dozens of uncommitted files sitting in working trees.
2. **Rebase Onto Main Post-Merge:**
   Once `main` is updated:
   ```bash
   # From Argon worktree
   git fetch origin && git rebase main
   
   # From Fable worktree
   git fetch origin && git rebase main
   ```
3. **Never Force Push (`--force`):** Always use standard fast-forward or non-interactive merge commits to preserve both models' execution history.
